{"task_id": "HumanEval/33", "solution": "```python\ndef sort_third(l: list):\n    \"\"\"\n    This function takes a list l and returns a list l' such that\n    l' is identical to l in the indicies that are not divisible by three, while its values at the indicies that are divisible by three are equal\n    to the values of the corresponding indicies of l, but sorted.\n    \"\"\"\n    l_prime = list(l)\n    divisible_by_three_elements = []\n    divisible_by_three_indices = []\n\n    for i in range(len(l)):\n        if i % 3 == 0:\n            divisible_by_three_elements.append(l[i])\n            divisible_by_three_indices.append(i)\n\n    divisible_by_three_elements.sort()\n\n    for j in range(len(divisible_by_three_elements)):\n        original_index = divisible_by_three_indices[j]\n        sorted_value = divisible_by_three_elements[j]\n        l_prime[original_index] = sorted_value\n\n    return l_prime\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/32", "solution": "```python\nimport math\n\n\n# In file: src/polynomial_zero_finder_errors.py\nclass InvalidInputError(Exception):\n    \"\"\"\n    Exception raised for invalid inputs to the polynomial zero finder.\n    This signifies violations of coefficient count or non-zero coefficient rules.\n    \"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, message: str):\n        self.message = message\n        super().__init__(self.message)\n\n\n# In file: src/polynomial_zero_finder.py\n\n\ndef poly(xs: list, x: float):\n    \"\"\"\n    Evaluates polynomial with coefficients xs at point x.\n    return xs[0] + xs[1] * x + xs[1] * x^2 + .... xs[n] * x^n\n    \"\"\"\n    return sum([coeff * math.pow(x, i) for i, coeff in enumerate(xs)])\n\n\nclass Polynomial:\n    \"\"\"\n    Encapsulates polynomial coefficients and provides evaluation functionality.\n    It serves as a domain entity for polynomial representation.\n    \"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, coefficients: list[float]):\n        self.coefficients = coefficients\n\n    def evaluate(self, x: float) -> float:\n        \"\"\"\n        Evaluates the polynomial at a given point x using the global poly function.\n        This delegates the core calculation to a defined utility.\n        \"\"\"\n        return poly(self.coefficients, x)\n\n\nclass InputValidatorModule:\n    \"\"\"\n    Validates polynomial coefficients against specified non-functional requirements.\n    It checks for an even number of coefficients and the presence of a non-zero term.\n    \"\"\"\n    def validate_coefficients(self, xs: list) -> None:\n        \"\"\"\n        Checks if the coefficient list meets NFR1 (even count) and NFR2 (largest non-zero coefficient).\n        Raises InvalidInputError if validation fails for either requirement.\n        \"\"\"\n        if len(xs) % 2 != 0:\n            raise InvalidInputError(\"Input coefficients list must contain an even number of coefficients (NFR1).\")\n        if not any(coeff != 0 for coeff in xs):\n            raise InvalidInputError(\"Input coefficients list must have at least one non-zero coefficient (NFR2).\")\n\n\nclass ZeroFinderModule:\n    \"\"\"\n    Orchestrates the process of finding a single zero point for a polynomial.\n    It utilizes validation, polynomial representation, and a numerical solver.\n    \"\"\"\n    def find_zero_internal(self, xs: list) -> float:\n        \"\"\"\n        Internal method to find a zero point for the polynomial defined by coefficients xs.\n        It encapsulates the numerical root-finding algorithm, adhering to FR2 and FR3.\n        \"\"\"\n        validator = InputValidatorModule()\n        validator.validate_coefficients(xs)\n\n        polynomial_instance = Polynomial(xs)\n        tolerance = 1e-6  # Precision for the returned zero point (NFR3).\n\n        # Initial interval for bisection. Given NFR1 & NFR2, an odd-degree polynomial\n        # always has at least one real root. We search for an interval [low, high] where poly(low) * poly(high) < 0.\n        low = -1.0\n        high = 1.0\n        val_low = polynomial_instance.evaluate(low)\n        val_high = polynomial_instance.evaluate(high)\n\n        # Expand the interval until a sign change is found, ensuring a bracketing interval.\n        # This loop robustly handles cases where roots are outside initial [-1, 1] range.\n        max_search_expansions = 100\n        expansion_factor = 2.0\n\n        for _ in range(max_search_expansions):\n            if val_low * val_high < 0:\n                break\n\n            low *= expansion_factor\n            high *= expansion_factor\n            val_low = polynomial_instance.evaluate(low)\n            val_high = polynomial_instance.evaluate(high)\n\n            # Prevent excessively large bounds; for odd-degree polynomials, a sign change is guaranteed.\n            if abs(high - low) > 1e15: # Arbitrary large number to prevent infinite expansion\n                low = -1e9 # Fallback to a very wide, fixed interval\n                high = 1e9\n                val_low = polynomial_instance.evaluate(low)\n                val_high = polynomial_instance.evaluate(high)\n                if val_low * val_high < 0:\n                    break\n                else: # Should not occur under valid inputs per NFRs\n                    return 0.0 # Return a default if a root couldn't be bracketed.\n\n        # Bisection Method for finding the root within the bracketed interval.\n        max_bisection_iterations = 1000\n\n        for _ in range(max_bisection_iterations):\n            mid = (low + high) / 2\n            val_mid = polynomial_instance.evaluate(mid)\n\n            if abs(val_mid) < tolerance:\n                return mid\n\n            if val_low * val_mid < 0:  # Root is in [low, mid]\n                high = mid\n                val_high = val_mid\n            else:  # Root is in [mid, high]\n                low = mid\n                val_low = val_mid\n\n        # If iterations complete without reaching desired tolerance, return the midpoint.\n        return (low + high) / 2\n\n\ndef find_zero(xs: list) -> float:\n    \"\"\" xs are coefficients of a polynomial.\n    find_zero find x such that poly(x) = 0.\n    find_zero returns only only zero point, even if there are many.\n    Moreover, find_zero only takes list xs having even number of coefficients\n    and largest non zero coefficient as it guarantees\n    a solution.\n    >>> round(find_zero([1, 2]), 2) # f(x) = 1 + 2x\n    -0.5\n    >>> round(find_zero([-6, 11, -6, 1]), 2) # (x - 1) * (x - 2) * (x - 3) = -6 + 11x - 6x^2 + x^3\n    1.0\n    \"\"\"\n    zero_finder = ZeroFinderModule()\n    return zero_finder.find_zero_internal(xs)\n\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/25", "solution": "```python\nimport math\nfrom typing import List\n\ndef factorize(n: int) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\" Return list of prime factors of given integer in the order from smallest to largest.\n    Each of the factors should be listed number of times corresponding to how many times it appeares in factorization.\n    Input number should be equal to the product of all factors\n    >>> factorize(8)\n    [2, 2, 2]\n    >>> factorize(25)\n    [5, 5]\n    >>> factorize(70)\n    [2, 5, 7]\n    \"\"\"\n    factors = []\n    if n == 1:\n        return []\n\n    temp_n = n\n    d = 2\n\n    while d * d <= temp_n:\n        while temp_n % d == 0:\n            factors.append(d)\n            temp_n //= d\n        d += 1\n\n    if temp_n > 1:\n        factors.append(temp_n)\n\n    return factors\n```\n"}
