{"task_id": "HumanEval/75", "solution": "```python\ndef _is_prime(n: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Checks if a number is prime.\n    Handles n < 2 and iterates up to its square root.\"\"\"\n    if n < 2:\n        return False\n    i = 2\n    while i * i <= n:\n        if n % i == 0:\n            return False\n        i += 1\n    return True\n\ndef _get_prime_factors(n: int) -> list[int]:\n    \"\"\"Finds all prime factors of a given integer.\n    Uses _is_prime to check potential factors and handles repeated factors.\"\"\"\n    factors = []\n    current_n = n\n    i = 2\n    while i * i <= current_n:\n        if current_n == 1:\n            break\n        if _is_prime(i):\n            while current_n % i == 0:\n                factors.append(i)\n                current_n //= i\n        i += 1\n    if current_n > 1:\n        factors.append(current_n)\n    return factors\n\ndef is_multiply_prime(a: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Determines if 'a' is the product of exactly three prime numbers.\n    Input 'a' must be less than 100.\"\"\"\n    if not (1 <= a < 100):\n        return False\n\n    prime_factors = _get_prime_factors(a)\n    return len(prime_factors) == 3\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/76", "solution": "```python\ndef is_simple_power(x, n):\n    \"\"\"Checks if x is a simple power of n (n^k = x for non-negative integer k).\n    Handles edge cases for x=1 or n=1 according to specification.\"\"\"\n\n    if x == 1:\n        return True\n\n    if n == 1:\n        return False\n\n    current_power = 1\n    while current_power < x:\n        current_power *= n\n\n    return current_power == x\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/76", "solution": "```python\nimport math\n\ndef is_simple_power(x, n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if a number x can be expressed as an integer power of another number n.\n    Returns True if x = n^k for some integer k, False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    # If x is 1, it is considered n^0 for any valid n (excluding n=0 if specific rules apply, but generally true).\n    if x == 1:\n        return True\n\n    # If n is 1, then 1 raised to any power is 1. Since x != 1 at this point, it cannot be 1^k.\n    if n == 1:\n        return False\n\n    # For the general case using logarithms, both x and n must be positive and n must not be 1.\n    # Non-positive x or n values (not covered by the above cases) cannot form simple powers in this context.\n    if x <= 0 or n <= 0:\n        return False\n\n    # Calculate the potential exponent k = log_n(x) = log(x) / log(n).\n    # Use a small epsilon for floating-point comparison to check if k is an integer.\n    epsilon = 1e-9\n    exponent_candidate = math.log(x) / math.log(n)\n\n    # Check if the candidate exponent is an integer within the tolerance.\n    return abs(exponent_candidate - round(exponent_candidate)) < epsilon\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/77", "solution": "```python\ndef iscube(a):\n    '''\n    Checks if an integer 'a' is a perfect cube.\n    It returns True if 'a' is a cube of some integer, False otherwise.\n    '''\n    if a == 0:\n        return True\n\n    # Determine the sign of the number to correctly handle negative cube roots.\n    sign = -1 if a < 0 else 1\n\n    # Calculate the integer cube root of the absolute value, rounding to the nearest integer.\n    root = round(abs(a)**(1/3))\n\n    # Reconstruct the potential cube with the correct sign and check if it equals the original number.\n    return (root * sign) ** 3 == a\n```"}
