{"task_id": "HumanEval/137", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import Any, Tuple, Type\n\ndef _parse_input(value: Any) -> Tuple[float, Type, Any]:\n    \"\"\"Parses an input value into a standardized float, preserving its original type and value.\n    Handles string representations with '.' or ',' decimal separators.\n    \"\"\"\n    original_type = type(value)\n    original_value = value\n\n    if isinstance(value, (int, float)):\n        numeric_representation = float(value)\n    elif isinstance(value, str):\n        # Standardize decimal separator from comma to period\n        s_value = value.replace(',', '.')\n        numeric_representation = float(s_value)\n    # The design assumes inputs are int, float, or str, and does not specify handling for other types.\n    # No explicit error handling is added as per instructions to trust caller's input.\n\n    return numeric_representation, original_type, original_value\n\ndef compare_one(a: Any, b: Any) -> Any | None:\n    \"\"\"\n    Create a function that takes integers, floats, or strings representing\n    real numbers, and returns the larger variable in its given variable type.\n    Return None if the values are equal.\n    Note: If a real number is represented as a string, the floating point might be . or ,\n\n    compare_one(1, 2.5) \u279e 2.5\n    compare_one(1, \"2,3\") \u279e \"2,3\"\n    compare_one(\"5,1\", \"6\") \u279e \"6\"\n    compare_one(\"1\", 1) \u279e None\n    \"\"\"\n    a_numeric, a_type, a_original = _parse_input(a)\n    b_numeric, b_type, b_original = _parse_input(b)\n\n    if a_numeric == b_numeric:\n        return None\n    elif a_numeric > b_numeric:\n        return a_original\n    else: # b_numeric > a_numeric\n        return b_original\n```"}
